      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-275751",
      ":question/problem": "A stick of length $6$ is broken at a point chosen uniformly at random along its length. Let the continuous random variable $X$ denote the distance from the left end of the stick to the break point, and let $Y = Y(X)$ denote the length of the shortest piece.\n\nExpressing the integrands in the following equation as polynomials, we have\n\n$E[Y] = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}$\n{{field-1}} $dx + \\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{6}$\n{{field-2}} $dx$\nTherefore, the expected length of the shorter piece is\n{{field-3}}."
